1
00:00:04,872 --> 00:00:06,558
This is the rule-design tool.

2
00:00:06,608 --> 00:00:07,737
It is a single file.

3
00:00:07,787 --> 00:00:14,248
you open it in a browser and it runs there, without installing anything and without sending data anywhere.

4
00:00:14,298 --> 00:00:17,287
It comes loaded with our eighteen-parameter panel.

5
00:00:17,337 --> 00:00:29,628
Each parameter has a header that summarises the state of its rule: the sigma metric, the combined false rejection rate, the same rate once an analyser offset is known, and the combined detection rate.

6
00:00:29,678 --> 00:00:32,976
Green means the criterion is met, red means it is not.

7
00:00:34,919 --> 00:00:37,418
The table has one row per control level.

8
00:00:37,468 --> 00:00:41,992
The white boxes are inputs: target, CV, bias, number of controls and K.

9
00:00:42,042 --> 00:00:44,671
Everything else is calculated as you type.

10
00:00:44,721 --> 00:00:56,890
The columns are the same as in the spreadsheet: sigma, the critical shift, false rejection, detection, the action limit in concentration units and the SD to enter in the laboratory system.

11
00:00:56,940 --> 00:01:06,495
Two columns are new: the analyser's offset from the target in SD units, and the false rejection rate you actually get once that offset is included.

12
00:01:08,487 --> 00:01:11,647
Here is the point of the whole study, in one control.

13
00:01:11,697 --> 00:01:21,091
This strip lists every allowable-error source we could trace for MCHC, computed from the same biological-variation data or taken from the published limit.

14
00:01:21,141 --> 00:01:23,930
Choose the tightest, and sigma falls below one.

15
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:27,818
the critical shift is negative, and detection collapses to zero.

16
00:01:27,868 --> 00:01:32,818
The noise of the method already exceeds the allowable error, and no rule can work.

17
00:01:32,868 --> 00:01:40,780
Choose the external assessment limit, and the same analyser, the same CV and the same rule sit at sigma seven with full detection.

18
00:01:40,830 --> 00:01:43,064
Nothing about the measurement changed.

19
00:01:43,114 --> 00:01:44,669
Only the specification did.

20
00:01:44,719 --> 00:01:48,681
The tool shows you the consequence before you commit to a source.

21
00:01:50,783 --> 00:01:53,765
You can set K by hand and see the effect immediately.

22
00:01:53,815 --> 00:01:59,595
Tighten level one to two SD, and the false rejection rate for the event jumps to almost ten per cent.

23
00:01:59,645 --> 00:02:00,761
the header turns red.

24
00:02:00,811 --> 00:02:02,511
Or you can let the tool search.

25
00:02:02,561 --> 00:02:13,006
The search looks for the rule set that stays within the false rejection budget, one and a half per cent per event by default, and meets the detection requirement for that parameter.

26
00:02:13,056 --> 00:02:20,236
Among those it picks the one closest to the rule you already have, so it does not rearrange a scheme that is already working.

27
00:02:20,286 --> 00:02:25,425
Here it moves level one only as far as it must, and pays for it by widening the other two.

28
00:02:25,475 --> 00:02:29,040
The meter shows how much of the budget each level is spending.

29
00:02:31,116 --> 00:02:33,264
The tool is not limited to our panel.

30
00:02:33,314 --> 00:02:38,575
Export your control results with analyte, level, lot, analyser and value, and load them.

31
00:02:38,625 --> 00:02:45,535
For each parameter and level it computes the pooled within-lot CV of each analyser and carries the highest forward.

32
00:02:45,585 --> 00:02:48,953
sets the target to the pooled mean of all your analysers.

33
00:02:49,003 --> 00:02:52,616
and reports how far the worst analyser sits from that target.

34
00:02:52,666 --> 00:03:03,605
That offset is written into the bias field, so the false rejection rate you see is the one you will actually get, not the one you would get if every analyser were perfectly centred.

35
00:03:03,655 --> 00:03:13,861
For MCHC, the worst analyser sits zero point eight SD from the pooled target, and the level-one false rejection rate with that offset is several times the nominal rate.

36
00:03:16,004 --> 00:03:22,989
Now suppose the target were the value the laboratory system had in force, 32.5, instead of the pooled mean of 32.98.

37
00:03:23,039 --> 00:03:28,801
The worst analyser would then sit almost half a unit further away, about two point six per cent.

38
00:03:28,851 --> 00:03:30,270
Enter that as the offset.

39
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:39,140
The rule has not changed, the CV has not changed, and the plain false rejection column does not move, but the rate with the offset climbs sharply.

40
00:03:39,190 --> 00:03:51,070
That is what happened in our retrospective test: the same rules rejected two and a half per cent of control events against the old targets, and under one per cent once the targets were re-centred.

41
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:53,823
The tool lets you see that before it happens.

42
00:03:55,895 --> 00:03:57,660
When you are satisfied, export.

43
00:03:57,710 --> 00:04:07,276
The file has one row per level with the rule, the action limit, and the SD to enter in your laboratory system so that its own three-SD rule lands on your limit.

44
00:04:07,326 --> 00:04:16,227
The Method and references tab inside the tool documents every calculation, with the same formulas as the spreadsheet and the references behind them.
